\section{The design formula (an item x step layout on the log scale)}{

\code{design} describes the step discriminations of the items as a two-way
item x step layout, read on the log scale like a two-way ANOVA:
\deqn{\log a_{il} = \mu + \alpha_i + \gamma_l + (\alpha \gamma)_{il}.}\tabular{llll}{
   \code{design} \tab Model \tab \eqn{a_{il}} \tab Fitted with \cr
   \code{~ 1} \tab PCM (common slope) \tab \eqn{a} \tab \code{tam.mml.3pl(E = tppcm(dat, design = ~ 1))} \cr
   \code{~ item} \tab GPCM \tab \eqn{\alpha_i} \tab \code{tppcm(dat, design = ~ item)} \cr
   \code{~ step} \tab step model \tab \eqn{\gamma_l}, common to all items \tab \code{tppcm(dat, design = ~ step)} \cr
   \code{~ item + step} \tab Rank-1 (product form) \tab \eqn{\alpha_i \gamma_l} \tab \code{\link[=xxirt_tppcm]{xxirt_tppcm()}} (not linear) \cr
   \code{~ item * step} \tab saturated TPPCM (default) \tab \eqn{a_{il}} \tab \code{tppcm(dat)} \cr
}


\code{~ item:step} and \code{~ item + step + item:step} are the same model as
\code{~ item * step}. The models form a hierarchy: \code{~ 1} is contained in
\code{~ item} and \code{~ step}, both are contained in \code{~ item + step}, which is
contained in \code{~ item * step}. Read as main effects and an interaction:
\itemize{
\item the item x step interaction means that items differ in the \emph{shape} of
their step discriminations: the ratios \eqn{a_{il} / a_{il'}} differ
between items (saturated TPPCM);
\item Rank-1 (main effects only): every item has the same shape
\eqn{\gamma_l}, scaled to its own level \eqn{\alpha_i};
\item GPCM (\code{~ item}): the shape is flat, every step of an item has the
item's discrimination;
\item \code{~ step}: the same shape and the same level for all items.
}

Accordingly the score test of the GPCM against Rank-1
(\code{score_test(gpcm_fit, against = ~ item + step)}) asks whether there is a
step effect, and the test of Rank-1 against the saturated model
(\code{score_test(rank1_fit)}) whether there is an item x step interaction.
Rank-1 is multiplicative, so it cannot be written as a linear design for
TAM; \code{tppcm()} stops and points to \code{\link[=xxirt_tppcm]{xxirt_tppcm()}}. For the four linear
models the log scale does not matter (the same equality restrictions on
\eqn{a_{il}}), and \code{tppcm()} returns the design array. The log scale
presumes \eqn{a_{il} > 0}: Yu (1991) restricted the step discriminations
to be positive, TAM does not impose it (\code{\link[=xxirt_tppcm]{xxirt_tppcm()}} does, with
\code{lower0 = TRUE}).

The same item x step language describes the locations in TAM's and
ConQuest's facet formulas: \code{formulaA = ~ item + step} is the rating scale
model (step parameters common to all items) and \code{~ item + item:step} the
partial credit model. \code{design = ~ step} is the rating-scale idea applied to
the discriminations.
}

\section{Custom restrictions}{

Any other linear restriction on the step discriminations is written into
\code{index}: steps with the same index share one parameter. \code{index} takes
precedence over \code{design}. To fix a step, use the \code{gammaslope.fixed}
argument of \code{\link[TAM:tam.mml.3pl]{TAM::tam.mml.3pl()}} with its index. Do not use
\code{gammaslope.constr.V} for equality constraints (it does not reach the
maximum likelihood estimate) and do not use \code{userfct.gammaslope} to impose
\eqn{a_{il} \ge 0} (truncation is not the constrained estimate); use
\code{\link[=xxirt_tppcm]{xxirt_tppcm()}} for bounds.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
set.seed(1)
dat <- sim_tppcm(500, disc = matrix(c(1, 1.5, 0.7), 4, 3, byrow = TRUE),
                      diff = matrix(c(-1, 0, 1), 4, 3, byrow = TRUE))
fit <- function(E) TAM::tam.mml.3pl(dat, E = E, est.variance = FALSE, verbose = FALSE)
m_sat  <- fit(tppcm(dat))                       # saturated: ~ item * step
m_gpcm <- fit(tppcm(dat, design = ~ item))      # GPCM
m_step <- fit(tppcm(dat, design = ~ step))      # step model
m_pcm  <- fit(tppcm(dat, design = ~ 1))         # PCM (common slope)
score_test(m_gpcm, against = ~ item + step)     # step effect?
# items 1-2 saturated, items 3-4 GPCM
idx <- rbind(1:3, 4:6, rep(7, 3), rep(8, 3))
m_mix <- fit(tppcm(dat, index = idx))
}
}
